.TH SCUF\-CONTROLLER 1 "September 2026" "scuf-controller 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scuf\-controller \- present a DualShock\-style gamepad as an Xbox 360 controller
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scuf\-controller
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scuf\-controller
reads a SCUF, DualShock or generic DualShock\-clone gamepad, takes exclusive
control of it, and re\-emits corrected events on a virtual input device that
identifies itself as a Microsoft Xbox 360 pad (USB 045e:028e). Games and
launchers that only understand XInput then see the expected buttons and axes.
.PP
Because the physical device is grabbed, its raw events stop reaching other
applications. This is what prevents the duplicated input these controllers
otherwise produce; no deletion of
.I /dev/input/js0
is required.
.PP
The daemon is normally run by the
.B scuf\-controller.service
systemd unit rather than invoked by hand. It waits for a controller to appear,
and reconnects automatically when one is unplugged and plugged back in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read configuration from \fIFILE\fR instead of \fI/etc/scuf\-controller.conf\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " " \fIPATH\fR
Use the event device at \fIPATH\fR instead of auto\-detecting one. Overrides
the config file.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " " \fINAME\fR
Use the named profile instead of auto\-detecting one. Overrides the config
file. See
.B \-\-list\-profiles
for the installed names.
.TP
.BR \-\-profile\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Load profiles from \fIDIR\fR instead of the system directories. May be repeated.
Use it to try a profile before installing it.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List detected gamepads with their device node, USB ids and the profile that
would be chosen, then exit. Does not require root.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-profiles
List the installed profiles and where each was loaded from, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-profile
Print a starter profile for the connected pad and exit. If an existing profile
partly fits, the output is prefilled from it and the rest marked TODO.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log every translated event, including ones that are unmapped. Use this to check
the mapping control by control.
.SH PROFILES
A profile describes one controller's layout. Profiles are read from
.I /usr/share/scuf-controller/profiles
and then
.IR /etc/scuf-controller.d ,
with a file in the latter shadowing a shipped file of the same name. The
profile name is the filename without its \fI.conf\fR suffix.
.PP
Each profile has an optional
.B [profile]
section holding a
.I description
and a
.I priority
tiebreaker, a
.B [match]
section deciding which pads it applies to, and
.BR [axes] ,
.B [buttons]
and
.B [digital-triggers]
sections describing the mapping.
.SS Matching
.B [match]
accepts
.I vendor
and
.I product
(four hex digits),
.I name
(a regular expression matched against the device name),
.I buttons
and
.I axes
(codes the pad must report), and
.I not_buttons
(codes it must not). Every stated condition must hold. Among the profiles that
match, the most specific wins: a USB id counts for far more than a name
pattern, which counts for more than a capability requirement.
.SS Mappings
.B [axes]
lines read \fIsource = target kind\fR, where \fIkind\fR is
.BR stick ", " trigger " or " hat
and decides how the source range is rescaled: sticks to \-32768..32767,
triggers to 0..255, hats to \-1..1.
.B [buttons]
lines read \fIsource = target\fR.
.B [digital-triggers]
lines map a button to a trigger axis, for pads that report a digital
"trigger down" bit alongside the analog value; the harder of the two wins.
.SS Adding a controller
.RS
.B sudo systemctl stop scuf\-controller
.br
.B scuf\-controller \-\-dump\-profile > /etc/scuf\-controller.d/mypad.conf
.br
.B sudo scuf\-controller \-\-debug
.RE
.PP
Correct the file against what
.B \-\-debug
prints, then restart the service. Adding the pad's USB ids to
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/60-scuf-controller.rules
stops it being enumerated alongside the virtual pad.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/scuf-controller.conf
Device selection, profile choice, stick inversion, and local button and axis
overrides applied on top of the profile.
.TP
.I /etc/scuf-controller.d/*.conf
Your profiles. Shadow a shipped profile by reusing its filename.
.TP
.I /usr/share/scuf-controller/profiles/*.conf
Profiles shipped with the package. Do not edit; they are replaced on upgrade.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-scuf-controller.rules
Hides the physical pad from gamepad enumeration so it does not appear
alongside the virtual one. Shadow it with a file of the same name in
.I /etc/udev/rules.d
to change or disable it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Identify the connected pads and the profile each would use:
.PP
.RS
.B scuf\-controller \-\-list
.RE
.PP
Try a profile without installing it:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scuf\-controller \-\-profile\-dir ./profiles \-\-debug
.RE
.SH NOTES
The virtual pad reports X as BTN_X (0x133) and Y as BTN_Y (0x134). Those codes
are aliases for BTN_NORTH and BTN_WEST, which is positionally backwards from
the modern semantic names, but it is exactly what the kernel's
.BR xpad (4)
driver does and what controller databases expect from an Xbox pad. Profiles are
written with the physical pad in semantic terms and the Xbox output in Xbox
terms, so a line such as \fIBTN_SOUTH = BTN_X\fR is correct and not a typo.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR udev (7),
.BR evtest (1)
